WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.020
How can nearest-neighbour TSP miss

2
00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.900
the best tour? We will

3
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.880
trace its locally cheapest moves,

4
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.020
include the final return edge,

5
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.980
and compare the completed route

6
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.660
with a much cheaper feasible

7
00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:13.120
tour.

8
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.700
Start at a specified vertex

9
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.920
and repeatedly choose its cheapest

10
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.500
unvisited neighbour. Keep a visited

11
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.240
set, move to the chosen

12
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.080
neighbour, and finally return to

13
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.820
the starting vertex once every

14
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:26.980
vertex has been visited.

15
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.540
Our four vertices are A,

16
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.160
B, C and D, with

17
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:32.340
A B and B C

18
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.420
both costing one. Edges A

19
00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:35.680
C, A D and B

20
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.320
D cost two, while C

21
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.000
D costs one hundred, making

22
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.200
one late continuation very expensive.

23
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.280
Start at A and choose

24
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.320
B, because its edge costs

25
00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.820
one rather than two. Mark

26
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.840
B visited, and consider only

27
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.340
vertices that have not already

28
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.940
appeared in the partial route.

29
00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.260
From B, C costs one

30
00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.440
and D costs two, so

31
00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.680
choose C. The partial route

32
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.040
A, B, C has cost

33
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.180
two, but its cheap prefix

34
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.980
has left only D unvisited.

35
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.120
The next move C to

36
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.820
D is forced and costs

37
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.180
one hundred. Then include the

38
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.800
final return D to A

39
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.060
costing two, giving a complete

40
00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.340
greedy tour of one hundred

41
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:17.080
four.

42
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.500
Now inspect the feasible tour

43
00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.420
A, C, B, D, A

44
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.840
instead. Its edge costs are

45
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.980
two, one, two and two,

46
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.240
for a total of seven,

47
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.380
strictly below one hundred four.

48
00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.280
One better feasible tour is

49
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.380
enough to show the greedy

50
00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.900
result is not optimal. We

51
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:37.420
do not need an exhaustive

52
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.300
optimality proof for the seven-cost

53
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.660
route to refute the claim

54
00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.300
that the one-hundred-four route is

55
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.060
always best.

56
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:45.440
A heuristic aims to find

57
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.320
a useful solution quickly without

58
00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.340
an automatic optimality guarantee. A

59
00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.520
proven approximation ratio is a

60
00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.960
different claim and requires explicit

61
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.000
assumptions and a theorem, not

62
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.640
merely a few successful runs.

63
00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.900
This graph does not satisfy

64
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.100
the triangle inequality, since the

65
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.060
C to D edge is

66
00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.660
far more expensive than travelling

67
00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.660
through B. Keep that limitation

68
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.700
explicit rather than using this

69
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.120
example to assert a theorem

70
00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.880
about metric TSP variants.

71
00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.660
Scanning all unvisited neighbours at

72
00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.060
each of n steps gives

73
00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.620
big O of n squared

74
00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.580
time and linear visited bookkeeping.

75
00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.980
The graph representation has its

76
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.100
own storage cost, and different

77
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.900
starting vertices or ties can

78
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.380
change the produced tour.

79
00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.860
On an incomplete graph, the

80
00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.380
simple rule can also get

81
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:34.920
stuck or fail to close

82
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.860
its tour. Our complete-graph assumption

83
00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.020
avoids that feasibility issue but

84
00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.280
does not repair its lack

85
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.040
of optimality.

86
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.620
Things to take note of:

87
00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.640
show the start, visited set,

88
00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.020
local comparisons and final closing

89
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.180
edge. Compare the whole tour

90
00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.660
with a concrete better route,

91
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:54.500
state ties and graph assumptions,

92
00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.060
and remember that a cheap

93
00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:57.700
beginning can conceal a very

94
00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.620
expensive ending, how charming.
